the heir apparent to the british crown  is usually known as the prince of wales  but did you know that the prince holds a  number of other titles as well  in order of precedence we first have the  title duke of cornwall  which is the oldest dukedom in england  having been created in 1337 and granted  to edward the black prince  unlike the prince's other titles this  one comes with specific privileges and  responsibilities  the title holder is given 150 000 acres  of land in southern england that land  and its assets net the prince 20 million  pounds a year  the next title is duke of rothsie  which is the title often used when the  prince is in scotland it was originally  held by the crown prince of scotland but  since the merging of the crowns of  england and scotland in 1604  the title is held by the prince of wales  the title earl of chester is directly  linked to the title prince of wales and  is generally given to the heir at the  same time as his princely title  next the titles earl of carrick and  baron of renfrew were traditionally held  by the prince of scotland and so are now  held by the prince of wales  one of the more interesting titles is  lord of the isles it was originally held  by the medieval leaders of these islands  the mcdonald's but in the 15th century  king james iv of scotland seized the  lands and the title lord of the isles  was thereafter granted to the scottish  prince  the last title somewhat strangely is  actually another princely one the prince  and great steward of scotland the two  parts of this title are technically  separate but are always combined in  legislation it traditionally had domains  over only a part of scotland the  principality of scotland  this video is a footnote of sorts for a  previous video of mine outlining the  history of the title prince of wales you  can watch that video here  you
 

